# 19. A stronger internal statement

I would revise the assessment’s final position to this:

> `RecallSegmenter` is a well-tested deterministic baseline, not a scientific segmenter. Preserving alternative cuts is also not a contribution by itself. Our target contribution is a joint narrative transduction model in which recall claims, discourse structure, proposition semantics, source anchors, fidelity modes, and recall trajectories are inferred together, and in which coverage, distortion, chronology, and population effects are marginalized over construction uncertainty. Existing methods establish many ingredients—latent event states, automated boundaries, semantic trajectories, embedding-based gist scores, and uncertainty calibration—but the close work examined here does not combine these into an evidence-backed, multigrain, open-world posterior over the source-to-recall transformation. That is a falsifiable candidate contribution, not yet an earned priority claim.

And the final sentence of the existing assessment is exactly right:

> If we merely replace the regex with a better cutter, we are following.

I would add:

> If we build a more ornate uncertainty ledger around the same hard segment-and-match analysis, we are also following.

The advance comes when the uncertainty **changes the inference and the estimand**.

